// The curve branch: the same edge pixels, described as arcs instead of lines.
//
// Run it over any directory of images -- no ground truth needed, because arcs
// are not scored (design-lab-model.md §11):
//
//   npm run lab -- --script pipelines/curves.lab --as linear \
//                  --out results/nut/ generated/nut/*.png
//   npm run overlay -- generated/nut/p0-l0.png results/nut/
//
// A is prepended by the runner, one per image:
//   A = load("<image>", from=srgb, as=linear)
//
// --as linear matters here for the same reason it does in geometry.lab:
// `gray` computes luminance, and the coefficients are only valid on linear
// values.

G  = gray(A)
B  = gaussian(G, sigma=1.4)
Gx = sobel(B, axis=x)
Gy = sobel(B, axis=y)
M  = sobel(B, axis=mag)
N  = nms(M, Gx, Gy)
S  = segments(N, Gx, Gy)
R  = merge(S)

// Everything above is geometry.lab unchanged. The branch is here.
//
// `merge` joins pieces that are the same LINE; `chain` joins pieces that are
// the same CIRCLE. A curve needs it because `segments` cannot grow along one:
// its straightness and direction tests each cap how long a piece can be, so a
// curve arrives in pieces short enough that a circle does not yet beat a line.
// On the nut, 72 of 74 labels after `merge` were still too short to prefer
// one -- on a subject that is mostly curves.
K  = chain(R)

// Two descriptions of the SAME label map, and running both is the comparison.
// `fit` describes every label as a line, because every label has one.
// `fitArcs` describes only the labels whose circle earned the extra
// parameter, and is silent about the rest -- those are the lines.
F  = fit(K)
AR = fitArcs(K)

// Compare K against R in the app, or with `npm run overlay`, to see what
// `chain` joined. That is why it is a separate operation rather than a flag.
